grammar => [
['Hedged urgency in UK register', q{<em>"It would be great if we could look at the numbers at some point — no rush, of course."</em> — the grammar of the wrapper: <strong>would-be-great conditional + at-some-point hedge + no-rush disclaimer.</strong> Three softeners, one real deadline. The reading rule: <strong>the more wrapping, the more weight on the middle</strong> — "look at the numbers" is the only load-bearing clause.}, q{"It would be great if we could look at the numbers at some point — no rush, of course."——包装纸的语法：<strong>would-be-great 条件句 + at-some-point 缓冲 + no-rush 免责。</strong>三层软化、一个真实期限。解读规则：<strong>包装越厚，中间承重越大</strong>——"look at the numbers" 是唯一的承重分句。}],
['The confirmation sentence', q{<em>"Just to confirm: Thursday."</em> — two words, one colon, zero register: the <strong>just-to-confirm frame</strong> is the universal decoder ring of cross-region work. It is polite in all three cultures precisely because it is registerless — the sentence makes no cultural claims at all. High-stakes dates deserve it every time.}, q{"Just to confirm: Thursday."——两个词、一个冒号、零语域：<strong>just-to-confirm 框架</strong>是跨区域工作的通用解码环。它在三种文化里都礼貌，恰恰因为它不带语域——这句话不做任何文化主张。高风险的日期每次都值得用它。}],
],
