tips => [
q{<strong>Observe, question, land.</strong> Ninety seconds fits exactly three moves. No pitch, no CV, no nervous laughter.},
q{<strong>Praise the work, specifically.</strong> "The one-page shape" proves reading; "great presentation" proves nothing.},
q{<strong>Ask real questions from your real work.</strong> The governance question cost nothing and produced the CIO\'s best answer of his morning.},
q{<strong>Declare your self-interest.</strong> "It means my joins keep working" — stated stakes are what make leaders treat you as a colleague.},
q{<strong>Take the invitation when it comes.</strong> "Send me the example" is a standing channel — following up once, precisely, is how eight-floor reputations compound.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'Prepare your three moves', p => 'For the most senior person you might realistically share a lift with: one specific observation (read their last note), one real question from your work, your exit line.', a => q{Keep the three moves on a mental index card, not a script — the lift gives you the opening, the moves give you the shape. Rehearse until each move is one sentence.} },
{ t => 'The stake declaration', p => 'Write your honest version of "X is the answer I was hoping for" for a current strategy question — including what it means for your actual daily work.', a => q{The daily-work clause is what elevates it: "it means my joins keep working" is checkable, concrete and self-aware. Strategy opinions without a daily-work consequence sound borrowed from the internet.} },
{ t => 'Follow up once', p => 'The CIO said "send me the example when the migration touches you." Draft the two-sentence follow-up email for that future day.', a => q{Model: "As discussed in the lift — the consolidation reached the churn dashboards today; the federated semantics held, and here is the one example you asked for." Sent once, precisely, months later — that follow-up is worth ten elevator rides.} },
],
},
{
num => 16, slug => 'pre-meeting-warmup', module => 4, read => 7, diff => '🟡 Intermediate',
title => 'Small Talk Before Difficult Meetings', cn => '硬会议前的暖场',
focus => 'Warm the room without biasing it: the neutral opener, the stake check, and the agreed tension',
scenario => [
q{Five minutes before a <strong>difficult meeting</strong> — the scope-cut conversation with the business sponsor. <strong>The sponsor arrives early.</strong> You now share a conference room and four minutes of unavoidable humanity with the person you are about to negotiate with. <strong>Skip the warmth and the room stays cold all meeting; fake the warmth and it reads as manipulation.</strong>},
q{距离一场<strong>艰难的会议</strong>还有五分钟——与业务赞助方的砍范围谈判。<strong>赞助方提前到了。</strong>接下来四分钟，你要和即将谈判的人共享一间会议室和无法回避的人性。<strong>跳过暖场，整个会冷到底；假装暖场，会被读成操纵。</strong>},
q{The craft is <strong>warming without biasing</strong>: a neutral opener (logistics, weather, the room), <strong>one stake check</strong> delivered honestly ("I know we are here to discuss a hard trade-off"), and <strong>naming the tension together</strong> — which paradoxically makes the negotiation gentler.},
q{手艺是<strong>暖场而不带偏</strong>：中性开场（后勤、天气、会议室）、<strong>一次诚实的立场确认</strong>（"我知道我们是来谈一个艰难取舍的"）、以及<strong>共同点破张力</strong>——这反而让谈判更温和。},
],
dialogue => [
['You (BA)', q{(sponsor arrives early) <strong>Neutral opener:</strong> "Morning — coffee\'s fresh, the room booking gave us an extra ten minutes." <em>Logistics, zero agenda.</em>}, q{（赞助方提前到）<strong>中性开场：</strong>"早——咖啡是新煮的，会议室多给了我们十分钟。<em>后勤话题，零议程。</em>}],
['Sponsor (Business)', q{<strong>Appreciated — my day is back-to-back.</strong> <em>I know why we are here, Wei.</em> The scope cut is not personal.}, q{<strong>多谢——我今天连轴转。</strong><em>我知道我们来这干什么，Wei。</em>砍范围不是针对个人。}],
['You (BA)', q{<strong>Stake check, honest:</strong> "I know it too — and I would rather say it plainly before the room fills: <em>this is a hard trade-off and both of us are defending something real.</em>"}, q{<strong>立场确认，诚实版：</strong>"我也知道——而且我宁愿趁人没到齐先说明白：<em>这是个艰难的取舍，我们俩各自守着真实的东西。</em>"}],
['Sponsor (Business)', q{<strong>Well said.</strong> <em>I am defending a regulator date; I assume you are defending the data quality.</em>}, q{<strong>说得好。</strong><em>我守的是监管日期；我猜你守的是数据质量。</em>}],
['You (BA)', q{<strong>Exactly —</strong> and <em>now that the stakes are named, the options will be easier to discuss than the positions.</em> <strong>Shall we agree the tension before the others arrive:</strong> <em>we are trading scope against dates against quality — no villains in the room.</em>}, q{<strong>正是——</strong><em>立场点破之后，讨论方案就比讨论立场容易了。</em><strong>其他人到之前先把张力说定：</strong><em>我们是在范围、日期、质量之间做交换——房间里没有反派。</em>}],
['Sponsor (Business)', q{<strong>No villains — agreed.</strong> <em>That sentence alone makes the next hour easier.</em> <strong>And thank you for the ten minutes of ordinary weather talk before it —</strong> <em>I needed my coffee more than I needed a cold open.</em>}, q{<strong>没有反派——同意。</strong><em>光这一句就 让下一个小时好过多了。</em><strong>也谢谢你之前那十分钟普通天气闲聊——</strong><em>比起一个冰冷的开场，我更需要那杯咖啡的时间。</em>}],
['You (BA)', q{<strong>The weather talk was not filler —</strong> <em>it was the human part of the negotiation arriving before the negotiation did.</em> <strong>Same time next month, more coffee.</strong>}, q{<strong>那十分钟不是凑数——</em><em>它是谈判中"人的部分"先于谈判到达。</em><strong>下月同时见，咖啡管够。</em>}],
],
phrases => [
['Neutral', q{Coffee is fresh, the room gave us ten extra minutes.}, q{咖啡是新煮的，会议室多给了十分钟。}, q{The logistics opener: zero agenda, zero bias, pure humanity. It buys the room temperature without spending anything.}],
['Stake', q{This is a hard trade-off and both of us are defending something real.}, q{这是艰难的取舍，我们俩各自守着真实的东西。}, q{The stake check: names the difficulty, honours both sides — it converts opponents into co-navigators.}],
['Tension', q{No villains in the room.}, q{房间里没有反派。}, q{The agreed-tension sentence — four words that disarm the whole meeting before it starts.}],
['Options', q{Now that the stakes are named, the options are easier to discuss than the positions.}, q{立场点破之后，讨论方案比讨论立场容易。}, q{The negotiation mechanics of pre-warming: positions are identities; options are just moves.}],
['Human', q{The weather talk was the human part of the negotiation arriving first.}, q{天气闲聊是谈判中"人的部分"先于谈判到达。}, q{Why the warmth matters: it is not delay — it is the relationship landing before the conflict does.}],
],
vocab => [
['Stake check', 'n. 立场确认', 'Naming honestly what each side is defending — before the room fills.'],
['Agreed tension', 'n. 共同点破的张力', 'Both sides confirming the trade-off exists — the negotiation\'s peace treaty.'],
['Cold open', 'n. 冰冷开场', 'Walking straight into positions with no human contact — the meeting you regret.'],
['Positions vs options', 'n. 立场与方案', 'What people defend versus what people can trade — warm rooms discuss the latter.'],
['Bias-free warmup', 'n. 无偏向暖场', 'Warmth that does not advantage either side — logistics, weather, coffee.'],
],
grammar => [
['The both-of-us frame', q{<em>"Both of us are defending something real."</em> — the <strong>both-of-us construction</strong> symmetrises the conflict before it starts: no attacker, no defender, two stewards. Compare "I know you have pressures" (asymmetric, patronising) — the both-frame keeps the meeting level.}, q{"Both of us are defending something real."——<strong>both-of-us 结构</strong>在冲突开始前就把它对称化：没有攻方、没有守方，只有两位守护者。对比"我知道你有压力"（不对称、居高临下）——both 句式让会议保持水平。}],
['Predictive softening', q{<em>"Now that the stakes are named, the options will be easier to discuss than the positions."</em> — the <strong>now-that + comparative prediction</strong> frame tells the room what will happen next, which makes it happen: naming the mechanism is half of installing it. Negotiators who narrate the physics get to choose the physics.}, q{"Now that the stakes are named, the options will be easier to discuss than the positions."——<strong>now-that + 比较级预测</strong>框架预先告诉房间接下来会发生什么，从而促成它发生：说出机制就是安装机制的一半。会解说物理场的谈判者，才有资格选择物理场。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Warm without biasing.</strong> Logistics, weather, coffee — the neutral band buys room temperature without spending advantage.},
q{<strong>Deliver the stake check before the room fills.</strong> "Both of us are defending something real" — said privately, it lands as honesty; said publicly, as theatre.},
q{<strong>Agree the tension explicitly.</strong> "No villains in the room" — the four-word treaty that makes hard conversations survivable.},
q{<strong>Move from positions to options.</strong> Named stakes turn identities into moves — that is the actual mechanism of the warm-up.},
q{<strong>Do not call it small talk.</strong> The four minutes are the human part of the negotiation arriving first — treat them with the same seriousness as the agenda.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'Your neutral opener kit', p => 'Write three bias-free openers for early arrivals at difficult meetings: one about the room, one about logistics, one about the day.', a => q{The bias test: would the sentence work identically whoever walked in? "The room gave us ten minutes" is neutral; "rough week?" assumes a state and privileges those who want to share it. Neutral means universal.} },
{ t => 'The stake check', p => 'For your next hard meeting, write the both-of-us stake check in two sentences, plus the agreed-tension close.', a => q{Model: "This is a hard trade-off — we are both defending something real: your regulator date, our data quality. Shall we agree there are no villains in the room before the others arrive?" Two sentences, one treaty.} },
{ t => 'The cold-open retrospective', p => 'Recall a difficult meeting that opened cold. Write the four minutes that should have preceded it — opener, stake check, tension agreement.', a => q{The retrospective is where the skill is born: replay the meeting\'s first hostile exchange and notice it was predictable from minute zero. The warm-up does not avoid the trade-off — it just ensures the trade-off is the only hard thing in the room.} },
],
},
{
num => 17, slug => 'topics-to-avoid', module => 5, read => 7, diff => '🟡 Intermediate',
title => 'Topics to Avoid and Graceful Exits', cn => '话题红线与优雅退场',
focus => 'The red band in practice: deflect, redirect, and leave without a door slamming',
scenario => [
q{The quiz night social. conversation drifts: someone\'s salary, someone\'s divorce, a manager\'s reorganisation leaks. <strong>Your red-band radar is pinging</strong> — and you are two messages from being quoted on Monday. <strong>Knowing the red topics is half the skill; the other half is the exit that leaves no wreckage.</strong>},
q{问答之夜社交现场。话题漂移：某人的薪资、某人的离婚、某经理的组织调整泄露。<strong>你的红区雷达警报大作</strong>——而你距离"周一被引用"只差两条消息。<strong>知道红区话题只是一半功力；另一半是那个不留残骸的退场。</strong>},
q{The toolkit: <strong>the deflection</strong> (answer the question behind the question), <strong>the redirect</strong> (hand the group a better topic), and <strong>the clean exit</strong> (leave on a completed beat, door open). <em>Grace is measured by what does not happen after you leave.</em>},
q{工具箱：<strong>偏转</strong>（回答问题背后的问题）、<strong>转场</strong>（递给全场一个更好的话题）、<strong>干净退场</strong>（在一个完整节拍上离场，门保持敞开）。<em>优雅与否，由你离开后没发生的事来衡量。</em>},
],
dialogue => [
['Colleague', q{So <strong>what do you actually make</strong> — is the bank band as low as they say?}, q{所以<strong>你到底挣多少</strong>——银行的带宽真有他们说的那么低吗？}],
['You (New Joiner)', q{(deflect) <strong>High enough to keep buying rounds!</strong> <em>But seriously — the bands are public on the intranet if the range matters; the personal number stays mine.</em> <strong>What is driving the curiosity — offer season?</strong>}, q{（偏转）<strong>够我请大家喝酒的！</em><em>不过说真的——带宽内网可查，如果区间重要的话；个人数字归我。</em><strong>是什么勾起你的好奇——要谈 offer 了？</strong>}],
['Colleague', q{(laughing) <strong>Offer season indeed.</strong> <em>Consider me deflected.</em>}, q{（笑）<strong>还真是 offer 季。</strong><em>我认输，被你偏转了。</em>}],
['Colleague 2', q{(lowering voice) <strong>And the rumour —</strong> is Elena\'s team really being restructured? <em>You sit near her.</em>}, q{（压低声音）<strong>还有那个传闻——</strong>Elena 的团队真要重组吗？<em>你坐她附近。</em>}],
['You (New Joiner)', q{(redirect) <strong>I sit near her coffee cup.</strong> <em>Whatever the org decides will arrive officially, and I would rather not pre-run the rumour mill.</em> <strong>Better question for this table:</strong> <em>who is winning the quiz league — because I intend to be on that team.</em>}, q{（转场）<strong>我坐她咖啡杯附近。</strong><em>组织怎么决定会正式公布，我不想预先启动谣言车间。</em><strong>给这桌一个更好的问题：</strong><em>问答联赛谁领先——我打算加入那队。</em>}],
['Colleague 2', q{<strong>Fair — the rumour mill can run without us.</strong> <em>Data team is second, engineering first.</em>}, q{<strong>有道理——谣言车间不缺我们俩。</strong><em>数据队第二，工程队第一。</em>}],
['You (New Joiner)', q{(later,话题 turning to a colleague\'s divorce) <strong>Clean exit:</strong> "This round is on me — <em>getting the next one in before the bar closes.</em> Daniel K, you owe me the airport story anyway — <strong>coming with?</strong>"}, q{（后来，话题转向某同事的离婚）<strong>干净退场：</strong>"这轮我请——<em>赶在酒吧关门前去买下一轮。</em>Daniel K，你还欠我机场故事——<strong>一起来？</strong>"}],
['Daniel K (Senior Dev)', q{(reading the room, standing) <strong>Following the drinks.</strong> <em>Nice save, by the way —</em> the table took the redirect without a wobble.}, q{（读场，起身）<strong>跟酒走。</strong><em>顺便说，漂亮的一手——</em>那桌毫无波澜地接住了转场。}],
['You (New Joiner)', q{<strong>The measure of the exit is the room you leave behind.</strong> <em>They kept talking — warmer, and without me in the blast radius.</em> <strong>Monday-me thanks tonight-me.</strong>}, q{<strong>退场的好坏由留下的房间衡量。</strong><em>他们继续聊着——气氛更好，而我不在爆炸半径里。</em><strong>周一的我感谢今晚的我。</strong>}],
],
phrases => [
['Deflect', q{The bands are public if the range matters; the personal number stays mine.}, q{带宽内网可查；个人数字归我。}, q{The deflection that answers the real question (the range) while refusing the private one (your number) — plus the curiosity question to move it along.}],
['Redirect', q{Better question for this table: who is winning the quiz league?}, q{给这桌一个更好的问题：问答联赛谁领先？}, q{The redirect hands the group a replacement topic — groups need somewhere to go, not just somewhere not to be.}],
['Refuse', q{Whatever the org decides will arrive officially — I would rather not pre-run the rumour mill.}, q{组织决定会正式公布——我不想预启动谣言车间。}, q{The rumour refusal: principled, impersonal, and said with a smile. It works because it cites the process, not the secret.}],
['Exit', q{This round is on me — coming with?}, q{这轮我请——一起来？}, q{The clean exit: a completed beat (buying the round) plus an invitation (the airport story) — you leave leading, not fleeing.}],
['Measure', q{The measure of the exit is the room you leave behind.}, q{退场的好坏由留下的房间衡量。}, q{The grading criterion for all social moves: what happens after you is the only durable evidence.}],
],
vocab => [
['Red band', 'n. 红区', 'Exact pay, active rumours, someone\'s visible struggle — closed channels.'],
['Deflection', 'n. 偏转', 'Answering the question behind the question while declining the one asked.'],
['Redirect', 'n. 转场', 'Handing the group a better topic — groups go where you point them.'],
['Rumour mill', 'n. 谣言车间', 'The unofficial news factory; refusing to feed it is a career move.'],
['Blast radius', 'n. 爆炸半径', 'Who gets hurt if the conversation is quoted — stay out of it.'],
['Clean exit', 'n. 干净退场', 'Leaving on a completed beat with the door open — grace, measurable later.'],
],
grammar => [
['The principled refusal', q{<em>"Whatever the org decides will arrive officially."</em> — the <strong>whatever-X-will-Y frame</strong> refuses by citing process, not by citing secrecy. "I cannot say" sounds like you know something; <em>"it will arrive officially"</em> sounds like you respect the channel. Same refusal, opposite side-lip.}, q{"Whatever the org decides will arrive officially."——<strong>whatever-X-will-Y 框架</strong>通过引用流程来拒绝，而不是引用保密。"我不能说"听起来你知道内情；<em>"会正式公布"听起来你尊重渠道。</em>同样的拒绝，完全不同的姿态。}],
['Exit grammar: completed beat + invitation', q{<em>"This round is on me — coming with?"</em> — <strong>[completed beat] + [invitation]</strong>. The beat buys you the moral right to move (you contributed); the invitation makes your exit a recruitment. Exits that only leave read as judgment on the room; exits that lead read as rhythm.}, q{"This round is on me——coming with?"——<strong>[完成的节拍] + [邀请]</strong>。节拍给你移动的道德权利（你贡献了）；邀请让你的退场变成招募。只离开的退场读起来像对全场的评判；带人走的退场读起来像节奏。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Answer the question behind the question.</strong> "The bands are public if the range matters" — the salary deflection works because it is generous AND closed.},
q{<strong>Groups need somewhere to go.</strong> A redirect with a destination ("who is winning the quiz?") beats a bare "let\'s not" every time.},
q{<strong>Refuse rumours by citing the process.</strong> "It will arrive officially" — you stay clean without implying you know secrets.},
q{<strong>Leave on a completed beat, with a plus-one.</strong> "Round\'s on me — coming with?" converts your exit from flight to leadership.},
q{<strong>Grade yourself on Monday.</strong> The measure of the exit is the room you leave behind — warmer, unquoted, and still glad you came.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'Red-band script kit', p => 'Write your three scripts: the salary deflection, the rumour refusal, and the redirect for a divorce-gossip table.', a => q{Each script needs the full grammar: deflection answers the real question; refusal cites process; redirect supplies a destination. Scripts rehearsed once land naturally; improvised red-band moves rarely do.} },
{ t => 'The blast-radius check', p => 'Before answering a sensitive question, run the check: who is in earshot, could this be quoted, would Monday-you approve? Write your personal three-question checklist.', a => q{Model: "1) Is anyone here the subject? 2) Could this reach them by Monday? 3) Would I say it if they were in the circle?" — the third question alone kills most red-band slips, because the honest answer is almost always no.} },
{ t => 'The exit rehearsal', p => 'At your next social, plan your exit in advance: the completed beat you will contribute, the invitation you will attach, and the moment you will take it.', a => q{Planned exits feel unscripted because the components are mechanical: contribute a round or a story (the beat), invite one person onward (the lead), and move at a natural completion (the moment). Grace is logistics wearing charm.} },
],
},
{
num => 18, slug => 'conversation-rescue', module => 5, read => 7, diff => '🟡 Intermediate',
title => 'Rescuing a Stalled Conversation', cn => '冷场急救',
focus => 'The three-second silence is a door: the observation pivot, the callback, and the honest out',
scenario => [
q{The coffee queue. You and <strong>Vivian</strong> have exhausted the weather, the weekend, and the printer. <strong>Three seconds of silence have arrived — and in queue-time, three seconds is a geological era.</strong> The conversation is stalling and you can feel both of you deciding whether to fake a phone call.},
q{咖啡排队中。你和 <strong>Vivian</strong> 已经聊完了天气、周末和打印机。<strong>三秒钟的沉默降临——在排队时间里，三秒等于一个地质纪。</strong>对话正在熄火，你能感觉到你们俩都在考虑假装接电话。},
q{The rescue toolkit: <strong>the observation pivot</strong> (something genuinely in front of you), <strong>the callback</strong> (a detail from earlier in the conversation or last week\'s), and <strong>the honest out</strong> — <em>"anyway, great to catch you"</em> which ends the chat with grace instead of pretending it never stalled.},
q{急救工具箱：<strong>观察转场</strong>（真正出现在眼前的东西）、<strong>回勾</strong>（本对话早前或上周的细节）、<strong>诚实的收尾</strong>——<em>"anyway, great to catch you"</em> 以优雅终结对话，而不是假装它从未熄火。},
],
dialogue => [
['You (New Joiner)', q{(three seconds in) <strong>Observation pivot:</strong> "That is the third person today I have seen carrying a plant through reception. <em>Is there a secret plant economy in this building?</em>"}, q{（三秒后）<strong>观察转场：</strong>"今天已经第三个人抱着植物穿过大堂了。<em>这栋楼里有秘密植物经济吗？</em>"}],
['Vivian (Hong Kong)', q{(laughing) <strong>There is — the moving desks, probably.</strong> <em>Half of floor six got reseeded last week; I am still waiting for mine.</em>}, q{（笑）<strong>真有——估计是搬家工位潮。</strong><em>六层一半的绿植上周换了新苗，我的还在排队。</em>}],
['You (New Joiner)', q{<strong>Callback:</strong> <em>You said your glasses fog on the way in —</em> does the humidity at least help the plants, or is the building fighting everyone equally?}, q{<strong>回勾：</strong><em>你说进门眼镜就起雾——</em>这湿度至少对植物友好吧，还是大楼对众生一视同仁？}],
['Vivian (Hong Kong)', q{<strong>Equal-opportunity hostility.</strong> <em>The plants get misted anyway; I get fogged.</em>}, q{<strong>一视同仁的敌意。</strong><em>植物有喷雾伺候；我只有雾。</em>}],
['You (New Joiner)', q{<strong>Second callback, further back:</strong> <em>Speaking of last week — did the Friday refresh survive the UK reporting season, or did the calendar of pain win?</em>}, q{<strong>第二个回勾，翻更旧账：</strong><em>说回上周——周五刷新挺过英国报送季了吗，还是"痛苦日历"赢了？}],
